---
import Admin from '../../../layouts/Admin.astro';
import Litany from '../../../components/Litany.svelte';
import '../../../styles/atelier/litany.css';
---

<Admin title="Litany" current="litany">
  <div class="litany-docs-overview">
    <p>
      <strong>Litany</strong> is a sample step sequencer. You search for sounds, build
      patterns on a step grid, and shape them with envelopes and effects. Everything
      is encoded in the URL — share the link to send the groove.
    </p>
    <p class="litany-docs-quick">
      <span>Add a voice</span> &rarr; <span>search for a sample</span> &rarr;
      <span>toggle steps</span> &rarr; <span>hit play</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <Litany client:only="svelte" />

  <div class="litany-docs">
    <details>
      <summary>Voices &amp; samples</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          A <strong>voice</strong> is a single instrument track. Each voice has a
          <strong>search query</strong> that pulls random samples from the catalog.
          The voice loads 8 random matches into a <strong>sample pool</strong> and
          rotates through them as the pattern plays.
        </p>

        <h3>Searching for sounds</h3>
        <p>
          Type a query into the search field (e.g. <code>kick</code>,
          <code>snare</code>, <code>808</code>, <code>vocal chop</code>). The
          field has an autocomplete with common instrument types. Press Enter or
          click away to trigger a search — the pool will reload with 8 random
          matches.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>re-roll</strong> button (<code>&#8633;</code>) refills the
          pool with the <em>same</em> query — you get different random samples without
          changing instrument type. The
          <strong>dice</strong> button (<code>&#127922;</code>) on the voice
          header picks a random instrument type (kick, snare, hi-hat, etc.).
        </p>

        <h3>Sample picker</h3>
        <p>
          The dropdown above the search field shows all 8 samples in the pool by
          filename. Selecting one <strong>pins</strong> it — the voice will always
          play that sample regardless of rotation settings.
        </p>

        <h3>Pin, mute, and solo</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Pin</strong> (<code>&#128204;</code>) locks the current sample
          so it never changes. <strong>M</strong> silences the voice.
          <strong>S</strong> solos it — all other voices are muted. Multiple voices
          can be soloed simultaneously.
        </p>

        <h3>Volume &amp; pitch</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>VOL</strong> controls the voice&rsquo;s output level (0&ndash;100%).
          <strong>PIT</strong> shifts the sample pitch &plusmn;12 semitones (one octave
          up or down). Pitch changes affect both tuning and duration — higher = faster,
          lower = slower. This is the same mechanism used in classic drum machines.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Step sequencer</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>step grid</strong> is a row of cells — one per beat. Gold cells
          fire the voice on that beat; dark cells are silent. Click any cell to toggle
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>step count</strong> dropdown (default 16) sets how many steps
          are in the loop. Every voice can have a different step count. Different
          lengths across voices create polyrhythms — a 16-step kick against a 12-step
          hi-hat will phase in and out.
        </p>

        <h3>Euclidean rhythms</h3>
        <p>
          Euclidean rhythms are a mathematical way to distribute pulses evenly
          across a pattern. Instead of manually clicking steps, you set three
          parameters and the algorithm generates the pattern for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Click the <strong>STEP</strong> button in the meta row to switch to
          <strong>EUCL</strong> mode. You&rsquo;ll see three sliders:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>P</strong> (pulses) &mdash; how many hits to place. Must be between
            1 and <strong>L</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>L</strong> (length) &mdash; the total number of steps in the pattern,
            1&ndash;32. This replaces the step count.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>R</strong> (rotation/offset) &mdash; shifts the pattern left by
            this many steps. Useful for finding the groove position.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The generated pattern appears as a read-only step grid below the
          sliders. Switch back to <strong>STEP</strong> mode to edit manually.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Common Euclidean patterns:</strong>
        </p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>P,L</th><th>Name / feel</th></tr>
          <tr
            ><td>3,8</td><td
              >Tresillo — the backbone of Latin and Middle Eastern music</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>4,16</td><td>Four-on-the-floor — kick drum in house/techno</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr><td>5,16</td><td>Bossa nova / clave</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7,16</td><td>Complex Afro-Cuban bell pattern</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5,8</td><td>Cinquillo</td></tr>
          <tr
            ><td>2,3</td><td
              >Hemiola — two against three. Try R=1 or R=2 for variation</td
            ></tr
          >
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Play styles</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each voice has a <strong>play style</strong> that controls how samples are
          triggered and stopped. Open the
          <strong>ENV</strong> panel and use the
          <strong>STYLE</strong> dropdown to change it.
        </p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Style</th><th>What it does</th><th>Use for</th></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>one-shot</code></td>
            <td
              >Sample plays to completion each time a step fires. Multiple
              overlapping triggers are allowed (polyphonic).</td
            >
            <td>Kicks, snares, one-shot FX</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>cut</code></td>
            <td
              >Each new step <strong>immediately stops</strong> the previous one.
              Only one trigger plays at a time (monophonic). No release ramp on choke.</td
            >
            <td>Hi-hats (open hat cut by closed hat), any monophonic line</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gate</code></td>
            <td
              >Sample plays while a step is active and <strong
                >stops with the release envelope</strong
              > when the step becomes inactive. Each step re-triggers the sample.</td
            >
            <td
              >Percussive sounds that shouldn&rsquo;t ring out, rhythmic gating</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>legato</code></td>
            <td
              >Like gate, but if consecutive steps are active the sample <strong
                >continues without re-triggering</strong
              >. Only re-triggers when coming from an empty step. Releases when
              the last active step ends.</td
            >
            <td>Sustained sounds, synth lines, vocal phrases</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Envelope</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>ENV</strong> panel controls how each trigger fades in and out.
          It has an interactive visual envelope display.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Drag the handles</strong> on the envelope curve:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Left handle</strong> (attack) — how long the sample takes to fade
            in from silence. 0s = instant. Max = 2 seconds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Right handle</strong> (release) — how long the sample takes to
            fade out. 0s = instant cut. Max = 3 seconds.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The area between the handles is the sustain portion — the sample plays
          at full volume here. For <code>one-shot</code> samples, the envelope is
          applied as a fade-in/fade-out over the full sample duration. For <code
            >gate</code
          > and <code>legato</code>, the release happens when a step becomes
          inactive.
        </p>

        <h3>Curve shaping</h3>
        <p>
          Each phase has a <strong>lin/exp</strong> dropdown:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>lin</strong> (linear) — a straight ramp from 0 to 1 (attack) or
            1 to 0 (release).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>exp</strong> (exponential) — an eased curve. On attack it starts
            slow then accelerates. On release it drops faster at first then tails
            off.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Preview</h3>
        <p>
          The <strong>&#9654; preview button</strong> plays the current pinned/active
          sample with the envelope applied so you can hear your settings. Enable <strong
            >auto</strong
          > to trigger a preview automatically when you adjust parameters (with a
          short debounce so you don&rsquo;t get rapid-fire playback while dragging).
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Effects (FX)</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each voice has a per-voice effects chain in this order:
          <strong>delay &rarr; reverb &rarr; filter</strong>. Open the
          <strong>FX</strong> panel to adjust. All effects are applied to the voice
          only — the master section has global controls.
        </p>

        <h3>Delay</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>TIME</strong> — delay interval, 10ms to 1 second</li>
          <li>
            <strong>FB</strong> (feedback) — how much of the delayed signal feeds
            back in, 0&ndash;95%. Higher = more echoes
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>WET</strong> — dry/wet balance. 0 = no delay, 1 = full wet
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Reverb</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>WET</strong> — dry/wet balance. Uses a synthetic impulse response
            for a small-room sound. 0 = dry, 1 = full reverb
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Filter</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>TYPE</strong> — lowpass (LP), highpass (HP), bandpass (BP), or
            notch
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>FREQ</strong> — cutoff/center frequency, 80Hz to 20kHz
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Q</strong> — resonance. Higher = sharper peak around the cutoff
            frequency, 0.1&ndash;20
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Sample rotation</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each voice pre-loads 8 random samples in its pool. The
          <strong>rotation</strong> dropdown controls how often the pool advances
          to the next sample.
        </p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Advances</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/HIT</code></td>
            <td>Every time a step fires</td>
            <td>Maximum variation — different sample each hit</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/BAR</code></td>
            <td>At the start of each bar (step 0)</td>
            <td>Changes each loop — controlled variety</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/4BR</code></td>
            <td>Every 4 bars</td>
            <td>Long-form consistency, then a new sample for variation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>PIN</code></td>
            <td>Never — always plays the pinned sample</td>
            <td>You picked a specific sample you want to keep</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <p>
          The <strong>sample picker dropdown</strong> shows all 8 pool entries. Picking
          one is equivalent to pinning it. Use the re-roll button to get a fresh set
          of 8 random samples if you don&rsquo;t like the current batch.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Master section</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>MASTER</strong> bar at the bottom has global controls that affect
          the entire mix.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>VOL</strong> — master output level</li>
          <li>
            <strong>COMP</strong> — compressor with adjustable
            <strong>THR</strong> (threshold, -60 to 0dB) and
            <strong>RATIO</strong> (1:1 to 20:1). Collapses dynamics to make the groove
            punchier.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>&#128279; SHARE</strong> — copies a URL to your clipboard. The
            URL encodes the entire app state (voices, patterns, FX, BPM, envelope
            settings, euclidean parameters) so the recipient opens your exact groove.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Keyboard shortcuts</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <table>
          <tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr>
          <tr><td><code>P</code></td><td>Play / stop</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>&#8984;Z</code></td><td>Undo (last 50 actions)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>&#8984;&#8679;Z</code></td><td>Redo</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</Admin>

<details>
  <summary>Step sequencer</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      The <strong>step grid</strong> controls which beats each voice fires on. Click
      a cell to toggle it — gold means the voice will trigger on that step. The number
      of steps is configurable per voice (1&ndash;32) via the dropdown. Different
      step counts across voices enable polyrhythm.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Euclidean rhythms</strong> are an alternative to manual step programming.
      Click the
      <strong>STEP</strong> button in the controls row to switch to
      <strong>EUCL</strong> mode. You set:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>P</strong> (pulses) &mdash; how many hits to distribute
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>L</strong> (length) &mdash; pattern length (1&ndash;32)
      </li>
      <li><strong>R</strong> (offset) &mdash; rotate the pattern</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The algorithm distributes pulses as evenly as possible. E(3,8) gives you
      the classic tresillo; E(5,16) gives a bossa nova pattern. The generated
      pattern is shown as a read-only step grid below the controls. Switch back
      to <strong>STEP</strong> mode to edit manually.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Play styles</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      Each voice has a <strong>play style</strong> that controls how samples behave
      when triggered. Open the
      <strong>ENV</strong> panel to change it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Style</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>one-shot</code></td><td
          >Sample plays to completion. Polyphonic &mdash; overlapping triggers
          are allowed.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>cut</code></td><td
          >Each new step cuts off the previous one. Monophonic &mdash; like a
          hi-hat choke.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>gate</code></td><td
          >Sample stops when the step becomes inactive (playhead moves past it).
          Release envelope is applied.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>legato</code></td><td
          >Like gate, but consecutive active steps don&rsquo;t re-trigger
          &mdash; the sample continues through. Only re-triggers when coming
          from an empty step.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </table>
  </div>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Envelope</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      The <strong>ENV</strong> panel has an interactive envelope display. Drag the
      <strong>attack</strong> handle (left circle) to set fade-in time, and the <strong
        >release</strong
      > handle (right circle) to set fade-out time. The curve fill shows the shape
      in real time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each phase has a <strong>curve type</strong> dropdown:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>lin</strong> &mdash; linear ramp (straight)</li>
      <li><strong>exp</strong> &mdash; exponential curve (eased)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <strong>preview</strong> button (<code>&#9654;</code>) plays the
      current sample through the voice chain so you can hear the envelope.
      Enable <strong>auto</strong> to preview automatically when you adjust parameters.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>FX</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      Each voice has a per-voice effects chain: <strong>delay</strong>
      &rarr; <strong>reverb</strong> &rarr; <strong>filter</strong>. Open the <strong
        >FX</strong
      > panel to adjust.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Delay</strong> &mdash; time, feedback, wet/dry mix</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reverb</strong> &mdash; wet/dry mix (convolution with a synthetic
        IR)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Filter</strong> &mdash; lowpass, highpass, bandpass, notch with frequency
        and Q
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Sample rotation</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      The <strong>rotation</strong> dropdown controls how often the pool advances
      to the next sample:
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>/HIT</code></td><td>New sample every time a step fires</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>/BAR</code></td><td
          >New sample at the start of each loop cycle</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td><code>/4BR</code></td><td>New sample every 4 bars</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>PIN</code></td><td
          >Always plays the pinned sample &mdash; never rotates</td
        ></tr
      >
    </table>
  </div>
</details>

<details>
  <summary>Sharing &amp; shortcuts</summary>
  <div class="litany-docs-body">
    <p>
      The entire state (voices, patterns, FX, BPM) is compressed and stored in
      the URL hash. Click <strong>SHARE</strong> in the master section to copy the
      link.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Keyboard:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>P</strong> &mdash; play / stop</li>
      <li><strong>&#8984;Z</strong> &mdash; undo</li>
      <li><strong>&#8984;&#8679;Z</strong> &mdash; redo</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</details>

<style is:global>
  .litany-docs-overview {
    padding: 12px 16px 0;
    color: var(--lit-text-dim, #7d8590);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .litany-docs-overview strong {
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
  }

  .litany-docs-quick {
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--lit-text-faint, #5a6270);
  }

  .litany-docs-quick span {
    color: var(--lit-accent, #e6a817);
  }

  .litany-docs {
    padding: 8px 16px 24px;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--lit-text-dim, #7d8590);
  }

  .litany-docs details {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .litany-docs summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    padding: 4px 0;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .litany-docs summary:hover {
    color: var(--lit-accent, #e6a817);
  }

  .litany-docs-body {
    padding: 6px 0 4px 8px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--lit-border, #262c30);
    margin-left: 4px;
  }

  .litany-docs-body p {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }

  .litany-docs-body ul {
    margin: 4px 0;
    padding-left: 16px;
  }

  .litany-docs-body li {
    margin: 3px 0;
  }

  .litany-docs-body code {
    background: var(--lit-cell, #181d21);
    padding: 1px 4px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .litany-docs-body table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 8px 0;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .litany-docs-body th,
  .litany-docs-body td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 4px 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--lit-border, #262c30);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .litany-docs-body th {
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
    font-weight: 600;
    width: 20%;
  }

  .litany-docs-body h3 {
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 12px 0 4px;
  }
</style>
